; Room items: 45 records of 8 bytes, the things Jean can touch (hearts, crosses, checkpoints, levers, parchments...)
;
; Read by #R$8957 when a room is entered: for every record of the current room #R$896B makes an object slot through
; #R$897E, unless the item's flag byte is $FF (the item was taken or is finished). The table ends with the room byte
; $1A. Record:
; .
; +0 byte: room number
; .
; +1 word: address of the item's flag byte in the variables at #R$851B-#R$854B (copied to slot +6/+7); every item has
; its own flag, whose meaning depends on the type (taken, pulled, active checkpoint...) and which survives leaving
; the room
; .
; +3 byte: type, the index in #R$8A8F (0-11)
; .
; +4 byte: row of the top-left cell of the 16 x 16 pixel sprite (0-21)
; .
; +5 byte: column of the top-left cell (0-31)
; .
; +6 word: the action routine that the handler #R$A600 enters when it has tested whether Jean touches the item (slot
; +4). For parchments (type 5, handler #R$A822) the word is not an address: the low byte is the number of the first
; text line and the high byte that of the second line (strings 25-36 of #R$91A1), which #R$A836 copies to #R$850E.
; .
; Positions are in character cells. The lists give room, what it is, row and column, and the flag.
@label=RoomItems
b$8EDF DEFB $02      ; room 2: bell, row 1 column 5, flag $851B
 $8EE0 DEFW $851B
@isub=DEFB $04,$01,$05,low ActionBellOrLever,high ActionBellOrLever
 $8EE2 DEFB $04,$01,$05,$48,$A7
 $8EE7 DEFB $09      ; room 9: lever, row 18 column 27, flag $851C
 $8EE8 DEFW $851C
@isub=DEFB $09,$12,$1B,low ActionBellOrLever,high ActionBellOrLever
 $8EEA DEFB $09,$12,$1B,$48,$A7
 $8EEF DEFB $0A      ; room 10: lever, row 4 column 7, flag $851D
 $8EF0 DEFW $851D
